              "function_response": null,
              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "**AltaVista.com** is a major piece of internet history! \n\nLaunched on December 15, 1995, by researchers at Digital Equipment Corporation (DEC), AltaVista was once the undisputed king of search engines. \n\nIf you are feeling nostalgic or curious about what happened to it, here is a quick look at its legacy and what happened to the domain:\n\n### Why AltaVista Was a Big Deal\n* **The First Modern Search Engine:** Before AltaVista, web directories like Yahoo! were curated manually. AltaVista was the first \"full-text\" searchable database of the World Wide Web, powered by a fast web crawler called \"Scooter\".\n* **Unprecedented Speed:** At the time, its speed was mind-blowing because it ran on DEC\u2019s cutting-edge 64-bit Alpha computers. \n* **Pioneering Innovations:** AltaVista is credited with introducing the first-ever **CAPTCHA** to block bots, and it launched **Babel Fish**, the web's first popular free machine translation service (which could translate entire web pages instantly).\n\n### The Rise and Fall\nDespite its early dominance\u2014reaching tens of millions of hits a day by 1997\u2014AltaVista suffered from a series of corporate shifts. DEC was bought by Compaq, which tried to turn AltaVista into a cluttered \"web portal\" (similar to Yahoo! at the time) rather than focusing on its clean, fast search interface. \n\nBy the late 1990s and early 2000s, Google emerged with a clean homepage and its highly effective PageRank algorithm, quickly pulling users away.\n\n### Where is it now?\nIn 2003, AltaVista was acquired by Overture Services, which was almost immediately bought by **Yahoo!**. Yahoo kept the AltaVista search brand alive for another decade, but eventually, on **July 8, 2013**, they officially shut down the service for good.\n\nToday, if you type **altavista.com** into your browser, it will simply redirect you to **Yahoo Search**.",
